{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BQYvba9d55HXFFuLnf7FNg","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis is the second half of conflict rounds. It adds a no-commit merge helper in gitx, a merge-parent option with guards in the committer code lane, conflict input and a merge-relative diff guard in the worker, and it turns on part A's switch in the starter config and servecmd. Part A (#322) has already landed on main, so the work can start now. The engineer is the only triage role that exists, so it gets the plan and the engineer worker does the work.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: implementation steps and a test plan across gitx, committer, worker, servecmd, the starter config and the proposal doc, using the earlier umbrella plan for #255 as background.\n- worker engineer: Go code change with unit and end-to-end tests that use the fakes.\n\n## Open issues\n- This change touches security: it alters the committer's path guard, staged-change check and lease-push handling for two-parent commits. A maintainer should review the PR closely, beyond what eng-review covers.\n- The conflict-round section of the worker prompt is out of scope here and needs its own human PR, which must merge with or before this one. Otherwise the switch turns on while the worker has no prompt guidance for conflict rounds.\n- The repository has no architect triage role. The cross-package design rests on the existing umbrella plan and the engineer's section.\n.ard:\n138\n142\n157\n158\n159\n160\n161\n165\n168\n174\n178\n184\n194\n203\n204\n205\n212\n213\n215\n216\n217\n221\n222\n223\n224\n227\n231\n236\n237\n238\n239\n241\n243\n244\n247\n252\n254\n255\n267\n268\n276\n292\n293\n297\n298\n299\n300\n302\n304\n305\n306\n307\n312\n313\n314\n322\n323\n332\n333\n334\n335\n336\n337\n338\n339\n40\n41\n42\n43\n44\n45\n47\n48\n49\n50\n51\n52\n56\n58\n59\n61\n89\nconfig.yaml\nreviewers\nscanners\ntriage\nworkers\n\n.ard/255:\ncosts\nlogs\nplan.md\ntriage-engineer.md\ntriage-lead.md\nworker-engineer.md\n1:# Plan for #255\n2:\n3:## Implementation\n4:\n5:### Steps\n6:\n7:Paths are under `internal/`. \"New\" marks anything that does not exist yet. Do not touch anything under `.ard/` (the worker prompt section is a separate human PR).\n8:\n9:1. `reviewfix/round.go`, `reviewfix/loop.go`: make `capPath` reason-aware. Add a `reason string` parameter (callers in `handle` and `landing` pass `ReasonReview`; the conflict path passes `ReasonConflict`), write it into the capped `Round` instead of the hard-coded `ReasonReview`, and word the cap comment for conflicts (\"Conflict rounds stopped ... the limit of N review rounds is reached\"). Keep the review wording and marker unchanged for `ReasonReview`. Reason: the cap comment currently talks about reviewers asking for changes. `RequestRound` passes `r.Reason` through.\n10:2. `merger/loop.go` (Deps): add an interface `Rounds interface { RequestRound(ctx, reviewfix.Request) (bool, error) }` (new) and a `Rounds` field. Nil means the old needs-human behaviour, so existing tests keep passing. `servecmd/merger.go`: pass the reviewfix loop. `servecmd/reviewfix.go` builds it now, so build it once in `servecmd` and share it between both loops. Reason: the merger must call `RequestRound`.\n11:3. `merger/merge.go`: new method `(t *tick) conflict(ctx, pr, issue, roles)` replacing the two `needsHuman(ctx, pr, \"conflict\", \"\")` calls (gate g, around line 121, and `updateBranch`, around line 317). Both are already limited to a computed conflict (`!*Mergeable || MergeableState == \"dirty\"`); keep 405 and unknown going to retry. Behaviour:\n12:   - resolve the worker role (`prgate.ParseWorkerBranch(pr.HeadRef)`) and its `max_review_rounds`;\n13:   - if the issue's newest round is already `requested` for this head (`reviewfix.Rounds`), log and return (idempotent);\n14:   - if the newest round is `capped` at this head, a human owns it: return without a new comment;\n15:   - dry-run: log `merge.would_request_conflict_round` and return;\n16:   - otherwise call `Rounds.RequestRound(ctx, reviewfix.Request{Issue, PR, Head: pr.HeadSHA, Worker, Reason: reviewfix.ReasonConflict, Roles: roles, Max})`. At the cap `RequestRound` already runs `capPath` (comment, needs-human, capped event); the merger adds nothing. On error call `t.fail`.\n17:   - when `Rounds` is nil or the branch is not a worker branch, fall back to `needsHuman(\"conflict\")`; update its text (merge.go:264) to say conflict rounds are tried first.\n18:   Update `merger/doc.go` gate 3.\n19:4. `gitx` (new `gitx/merge.go`): add `(g *Git) MergeNoCommit(ctx, dir, ref string) (conflicted []string, err error)` running `git merge --no-commit --no-ff <ref>` and returning the unmerged paths (`git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U`); exit 1 with unmerged paths is not an error. Also `UnmergedPaths(ctx, dir)` and `MergeAbort`. Used by both the worker and the committer.\n20:5. `committer/code.go`, `committer/committer.go`: the single-parent lane cannot push a merge, and a squash-only resolution would not clear GitHub's conflict (main would still not be an ancestor). Add `MergeParent string` (40-hex main tip, optional) to `CodeRequest`. When set, `attempt` starts from the fetched branch tip, runs `MergeNoCommit(MergeParent)`, writes the declared snapshot files over the conflicted paths, requires `UnmergedPaths` to be empty after staging, and commits (two parents) through `effects.CommitOp`. `CheckStaged(declared, changes)` must then accept the paths changed by the merge itself: compare staged changes against `MergeParent`'s tree for the undeclared check, and keep `CheckCodePath` on every path. Keep the lease and retry loop. Fail with `ErrConflict` as today if the branch moved. `codeSnapshot` validates `MergeParent` with `shaRe`.\n21:6. `worker/conflict.go` (new): `(l *Loop) conflictInput(ctx, role, n, branch, r reviewfix.Round) (executor.File, mainTip string, ok bool, err error)`. Fetch `main` to a private ref in `RepoDir`, then build `conflict.md`: begins with `agentjob.UntrustedHeader`, names the round, lists the conflicted files and includes the hunks (`git merge-tree --write-tree` output or a trial `MergeNoCommit` in a scratch worktree, then abort), capped at `feedbackCap`. It marks hunks as untrusted text from the PR and `main`. If the trial merge is clean (main moved), return `ok=false` so the worker just proceeds with the normal flow.\n22:7. `worker/feedback.go` and `worker/run.go` (lines ~114-125): when the newest requested round has `Reason == reviewfix.ReasonConflict`, add `conflict.md` instead of `review-feedback.md` (both marked untrusted). Record the main tip on the `job` (new field `mergeParent`). Before the agent step, merge `origin/main` into the job worktree with `MergeNoCommit` so the agent sees real conflict markers and runs `make test` on the merge result.\n23:8. `worker/run.go` `round` / `push` and `worker/guard.go` (`facts`, `Check`): in a conflict round, compute the diff guard against the merge result relative to `mergeParent` (the PR's own changes), not against `BaseSHA`, so main's changes do not trip `diff_max_lines`. Refuse the round (finish as needs-human) if any conflict marker (`<<<<<<<`, `>>>>>>>`) is left in a file or any path is still unmerged. Pass `MergeParent: jb.mergeParent` in `committer.CodeRequest` in `push`. The commit title and trailer stay as today. A role file with a non-done status (the worker cannot resolve confidently) already ends needs-human through `finishWithPR`; do not change that.\n24:9. `reviewfix/loop.go` `landing`: a conflict round lands when the head is a merge that includes `R.Head`. The `covered` check (no code changed, capped) must not misfire on a merge-only resolution: for `Reason == conflict` treat \"pr head has `R.Head` as ancestor and differs from it\" as applied (use `gitx` ancestor check, new `IsAncestor(ctx, dir, a, b)` if none exists), then remove every requested reviewer's `:done` and `:changes` labels and append the applied event (the existing code). If the head did not move, cap with reason conflict.\n25:10. `docs/proposal.md`: replace the \"Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up\" sentence (line ~332) and the conflict bullet in the merger section (line ~378) with the conflict-round behaviour (merge round, counts toward `max_review_rounds`, union conflicts resolved by the worker, overlapping logic ends needs-human, all requested reviewers re-run, kill switch and dry-run apply). Add a short paragraph on the worker's conflict round and on the merge-parent commit in the code lane (line ~160). Update the `reviewfix/doc.go` last paragraph.\n26:\n27:### Test plan\n28:\n29:Test command: `make test` (`go test ./...`), plus `make lint`.\n30:\n31:- `merger/loop_test.go` `TestConflictRequestsRound`: ready PR with `Mergeable=false`; fake `Rounds` records one `Request` with `Reason: conflict`, all requested roles, correct `Max`; no needs-human label or comment.\n32:- `TestConflictUnknownAndNotMergeableRetry`: `Mergeable==nil`, state `unknown` and a 405 from merge produce no round and no label.\n33:- `TestConflictRoundIdempotent`: newest round requested for the same head: no second request.\n34:- `TestConflictCapped`: `Requested` count equals `Max`: the real reviewfix `capPath` posts the comment, adds needs-human and appends a capped event with reason conflict; a review round and a conflict round together reach the cap (the cap counts conflict rounds).\n35:- `TestConflictDryRun`: no round, no label, a `would_request` log line.\n36:- `TestConflictNilRoundsFallsBack`: `Rounds == nil` keeps the old needs-human comment.\n37:- `TestUpdateBranchConflictRequestsRound` for the `updateBranch` 409 path.\n38:- `gitx/merge_test.go`: `MergeNoCommit` on a scratch repo with a union conflict returns the conflicted path; clean merge returns none; `UnmergedPaths` after resolution is empty.\n39:- `committer/code_test.go`: `CommitCode` with `MergeParent` produces a commit with two parents whose tree has the resolved files and main's other changes; refuses a leftover unmerged path; refuses a forbidden path (`.github/`) arriving through main's side only if declared by the worker; `ErrConflict` when the branch moved; invalid `MergeParent` is `ErrInvalid`.\n40:- `worker/loop_test.go`: `TestConflictRoundInput` (the input has `conflict.md` listed untrusted, carries the header, hunks and file names, is capped); `TestConflictRoundUnionResolved` (fake agent keeps both sides, `make test` passes, push has `MergeParent`); `TestConflictRoundOverlapNeedsHuman` (fake agent returns a non-done role file, job ends needs-human, nothing pushed); `TestConflictMarkersLeftRefused`.\n41:- `reviewfix/loop_test.go`: a conflict round lands when the head is a merge descendant, removes all requested reviewers' labels; a head that did not move caps with reason conflict.\n42:- End-to-end in `merger/integration_test.go` (fake GitHub, scratch git repo, fake agent): ready PR with a union conflict (two list entries added to the same place) goes conflict round, worker resolves, reviewers re-run and pass, merger merges with no human label. Second case: overlapping logic edit ends `ard:needs-human`.\n43:\n44:### Risk\n45:\n46:High. It crosses the merger, reviewfix, worker and the committer code lane (security-sensitive: path guard, `CheckStaged`, force-with-lease push). A merge commit brings main's files into the branch, so the guard must still reject forbidden paths and must not let the agent write outside the conflicted files. `landing` logic and `capPath` wording change on a shared state machine (concurrency with the worker claim and the `review_round` event order). Public interfaces: `committer.CodeRequest`, `reviewfix.capPath`, `merger.Deps`. No migration. No protected path in `merge.protected_paths` is edited; the worker prompt section is excluded on purpose, so until a human adds it the agent runs with the existing prompt plus the `conflict.md` input.\n47:\n48:### Size\n49:\n50:XL by the strict reading is avoided only by keeping each step minimal; estimate L (about 1,200 to 1,800 changed lines including tests, so expect a large-change note above diff_warn_lines). If it passes diff_max_lines, split into (a) merger request path plus reviewfix reason handling, and (b) merge-parent code lane plus worker conflict input.\n51:\n52:Files touched: `internal/merger/{loop.go,merge.go,doc.go,loop_test.go,integration_test.go}`, `internal/reviewfix/{round.go,loop.go,doc.go,loop_test.go}`, `internal/gitx/{merge.go (new),merge_test.go (new)}`, `internal/committer/{code.go,committer.go,code_test.go}`, `internal/worker/{conflict.go (new),feedback.go,run.go,guard.go,loop_test.go}`, `internal/servecmd/{merger.go,reviewfix.go}`, `docs/proposal.md`.\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nGuidance for Claude Code (and humans) working in this repository.\n\n## What this is\n\nard is a controller that automates repository development: scanners → triage → worker → reviewers → merger, coordinated through `ard:` GitHub labels. The design is [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md). Read the relevant section before changing behaviour.\n\n**docs/proposal.md is the source of truth for design.** A PR that changes behaviour updates the proposal in the same PR. If code and proposal disagree, raise it on the issue rather than silently picking one.\n\n## Invariants — never violate\n\n1. **The controller owns every side effect.** Only controller code calls GitHub or runs `git commit`/`push`/`merge`. Agents edit files in a worktree and report through `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` frontmatter; they never touch labels, comments or branches.\n2. **Agents hold no GitHub token.** Credentials stay in the controller and executor.\n3. **The main-branch committer writes only `.ard/<issue#>/`.** Config (`.ard/config.yaml`) and role directories (`.ard/<type>/<name>/`) are human-owned. Only `ard init` may write them: into the local working tree for a human to commit, or (`--pr`) on the `ard-init` branch through `committer.Init` and a PR a human merges; ard never writes main. Workers' diff guard, the committer's path guards and the merger's protected paths enforce it.\n4. **Every label is prefixed `ard:`** and follows `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]` (see the label table in the proposal).\n5. **Every side effect honours dry-run.** Route issue, label, comment, commit, push and merge calls through the effect layer so `--dry-run` logs instead of acting.\n6. **Untrusted text is data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text reach agents as files marked untrusted, never spliced into prompts.\n7. **Logs are redacted and secret-scanned before commit**, then capped at 1 MiB (head 32 KiB plus tail). The scan fails closed.\n\n## Stack\n\n- Go, a single static `ard` binary (CGO off; use a pure-Go SQLite driver such as `modernc.org/sqlite`).\n- Supported platforms: macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported (the local executor relies on `flock` and POSIX process handling).\n- GitHub access through a GitHub App installation token, behind an interface so tests use a fake.\n- The language choice is tracked as a decision issue; if it changes, update this file.\n\n## Layout\n\n[docs/layout.md](docs/layout.md) lists every directory and package and what it holds. Read it to find where code lives; a PR that adds, removes or renames a directory or package updates that file, not this one.\n\nNew subcommands: implement the body in its own package as\n`func(ctx context.Context, e *cli.Env) error`, then add one field to\n`cli.Handlers` and one wiring line in `cmd/ard/main.go`'s `handlers()`.\nA nil handler runs the command as a \"not implemented yet\" stub.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nmake build   # bin/ard\nmake test    # go test ./...\nmake lint    # go vet + gofmt check\nmake fmt     # gofmt -w\n```\n\nRun `make lint test` before every commit.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Tests: table-driven, no network. Use the GitHub fake for unit tests; real-API tests go behind the `integration` build tag.\n- Errors: wrap with context (`fmt.Errorf(\"claim issue %d: %w\", n, err)`); never swallow.\n- Logging: structured (`log/slog`), one event per claim, close-out and failure.\n- Keep packages small and interfaces at the consumer.\n\n## Workflow\n\n- Work is tracked in GitHub issues with `phase:N`, `area:*` and `type:*` labels and a phase milestone. Pick from the lowest open phase.\n- Branch per issue: `<issue#>-<short-slug>`. Open a PR that says `Closes #<issue>`. Do not push to `main` directly.\n- Keep PRs to one issue. Note follow-ups as new issues rather than widening the PR.\n- Decision issues (`type:decision`) record the choice in a comment, then update the proposal and close.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"893e03eb-e769-477f-943a-76987753ca98","uuid":"9356d5bd-e156-4566-b86b-5066e6f11a2b","timestamp":"2026-11-24T20:48:32.726Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis is the second half of conflict rounds. It adds a no-commit merge helper in gitx, a merge-parent option with guards in the committer code lane, conflict input and a merge-relative diff guard in the worker, and it turns on part A's switch in the starter config and servecmd. Part A (#322) has already landed on main, so the work can start now. The engineer is the only triage role that exists, so it gets the plan and the engineer worker does the work.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: implementation steps and a test plan across gitx, committer, worker, servecmd, the starter config and the proposal doc, using the earlier umbrella plan for #255 as background.\n- worker engineer: Go code change with unit and end-to-end tests that use the fakes.\n\n## Open issues\n- This change touches security: it alters the committer's path guard, staged-change check and lease-push handling for two-parent commits. A maintainer should review the PR closely, beyond what eng-review covers.\n- The conflict-round section of the worker prompt is out of scope here and needs its own human PR, which must merge with or before this one. Otherwise the switch turns on while the worker has no prompt guidance for conflict rounds.\n- The repository has no architect triage role. The cross-package design rests on the existing umbrella plan and the engineer's section.\n.ard:\n138\n142\n157\n158\n159\n160\n161\n165\n168\n174\n178\n184\n194\n203\n204\n205\n212\n213\n215\n216\n217\n221\n222\n223\n224\n227\n231\n236\n237\n238\n239\n241\n243\n244\n247\n252\n254\n255\n267\n268\n276\n292\n293\n297\n298\n299\n300\n302\n304\n305\n306\n307\n312\n313\n314\n322\n323\n332\n333\n334\n335\n336\n337\n338\n339\n40\n41\n42\n43\n44\n45\n47\n48\n49\n50\n51\n52\n56\n58\n59\n61\n89\nconfig.yaml\nreviewers\nscanners\ntriage\nworkers\n\n.ard/255:\ncosts\nlogs\nplan.md\ntriage-engineer.md\ntriage-lead.md\nworker-engineer.md\n1:# Plan for #255\n2:\n3:## Implementation\n4:\n5:### Steps\n6:\n7:Paths are under `internal/`. \"New\" marks anything that does not exist yet. Do not touch anything under `.ard/` (the worker prompt section is a separate human PR).\n8:\n9:1. `reviewfix/round.go`, `reviewfix/loop.go`: make `capPath` reason-aware. Add a `reason string` parameter (callers in `handle` and `landing` pass `ReasonReview`; the conflict path passes `ReasonConflict`), write it into the capped `Round` instead of the hard-coded `ReasonReview`, and word the cap comment for conflicts (\"Conflict rounds stopped ... the limit of N review rounds is reached\"). Keep the review wording and marker unchanged for `ReasonReview`. Reason: the cap comment currently talks about reviewers asking for changes. `RequestRound` passes `r.Reason` through.\n10:2. `merger/loop.go` (Deps): add an interface `Rounds interface { RequestRound(ctx, reviewfix.Request) (bool, error) }` (new) and a `Rounds` field. Nil means the old needs-human behaviour, so existing tests keep passing. `servecmd/merge